# FlatlineRoundtable — example config
#
# Copy to ~/.config/flatline-roundtable/FlatlineRoundtable.yaml and edit.
# That path is deliberate: your real config lives OUTSIDE the repo, so a stray
# `git add -f`, a stash, or an editor backup cannot publish it.
#
# NO SECRET VALUE EVER GOES IN THIS FILE. `key_entry` names a `pass` entry;
# the key is read into memory only while that lane is being queried.

defaults:
  max_tokens: 2000
  timeout: 300
  temperature: 0.7

concurrency: 8          # global default; lanes may lower it
retries: 1              # transient 5xx / connection errors only
deadline_seconds: 600   # whole-run wall clock
# budget_usd: 0.50      # refuse to finish over this (needs price_per_mtok on metered lanes)

lanes:
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------- http ----
  # Any OpenAI-compatible endpoint. `vendor` is a human label used for grouping
  # and per-vendor concurrency; routing depends on harness + base_url, not on it.
  - name: Skeptic
    active: true
    vendor: openrouter
    harness: http
    model: some-vendor/some-model
    base_url: https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
    key_entry: openrouter/agent/skeptic     # a pass ENTRY NAME, never a key
    price_per_mtok: 1.50                    # optional; enables spend estimates
    personality: |
      You are the Skeptic. Assume every claim is unproven until it is shown.
      Attack the weakest link in the argument, not the conclusion.

  # A free-tier model: same shape, no price.
  - name: Contrarian
    active: true
    vendor: openrouter
    harness: http
    model: some-vendor/some-model:free
    base_url: https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
    key_entry: openrouter/agent/contrarian
    personality: |
      You are the Contrarian. Argue the position the others are least likely
      to take, as long as it is defensible.

  # --------------------------------------------------------------- local ----
  # A llama.cpp / vLLM server on your own hardware. No key needed.
  - name: Local
    active: false
    vendor: local
    harness: http
    model: your-local-model
    base_url: http://localhost:8080/v1
    timeout: 180
    concurrency: 1   # llama.cpp with --parallel 1 serves ONE request at a time;
                     # fanning out against it queues every lane past its timeout
    personality: |
      You are the house model. Be concise and concrete.

  # ----------------------------------------------------------------- cli ----
  # Subscription-backed assistants, driven in one-shot print mode.
  #
  # THIS IS A COST BOUNDARY, NOT A STYLE CHOICE. These are $0 only through their
  # own OAuth session. Rewriting one as an `http` lane pointed at the vendor API
  # silently moves it onto metered credits, which bill separately from the
  # subscription. The tool scrubs ANTHROPIC_API_KEY / OPENAI_API_KEY and friends
  # from the child environment for exactly this reason.
  - name: Chair
    active: false
    vendor: anthropic
    harness: cli
    command: claude
    args: ["-p"]
    stdin: true      # this CLI reads the prompt from stdin — safest for long briefs
    model: opus
    personality: |
      You chair the roundtable. Weigh the strongest case on each side and say
      which one actually survives.

  # Arg templates rather than hardcoded per-CLI logic: some CLIs take the prompt
  # as an argument and consume the NEXT token as its value, so flags must come
  # first. Encoding that here means a CLI change is a config edit, not a patch.
  - name: Outsider
    active: false
    vendor: google
    harness: cli
    command: agy
    args: ["--model", "some-model", "-p", "{prompt}"]
    model: some-model
    personality: |
      You are the outsider. You have not seen this codebase before. Say what is
      confusing on first read.

  # ------------------------------------------------------------- disabled ----
  # Keep a lane in the file with active:false rather than deleting it, so the
  # reason it is off does not get lost.
  - name: Parked
    active: false
    vendor: example
    harness: cli
    command: some-acp-binary
    args: ["-p"]
    model: some-model
    notes: "no one-shot mode — protocol-over-stdio only; needs a wrapper first"
